I will implement `pLimit` by maintaining a queue of pending jobs and a counter of currently running promises. Each time a job is submitted, it is added to the queue and a worker is started if the number of running jobs is below the concurrency limit. When a job resolves or rejects, the worker count is decremented and the next job in the queue is started. This ensures jobs are processed in order, concurrency never exceeds `n`, and a rejection does not block subsequent jobs.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function pLimit(n) {
  let activeCount = 0;
  const queue = [];

  const next = () => {
    if (activeCount >= n || queue.length === 0) return;
    const { resolve, reject, fn } = queue.shift();
    activeCount++;
    Promise.resolve(fn())
      .then(resolve)
      .catch(reject)
      .finally(() => {
        activeCount--;
        next();
      });
  };

  return (fn) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    queue.push({ resolve, reject, fn });
    next();
  });
}

module.exports = pLimit;
```

