hjs-008.text:
I will build a tagged template function that walks the template literal parts and interpolations, building a SQL string with numbered placeholders. For each interpolation I will check its type: if it is another `sqlTag` result I will inline its text and adjust its placeholders by adding the current offset; if it is an array I will join its elements with commas and wrap the whole thing in parentheses, each element being a placeholder; otherwise I will treat it as a simple value and assign the next placeholder number. The function will collect all values in order and return an object `{text, values}`. Edge cases include handling `null`/`undefined` as empty strings, numbers and booleans as themselves, and ensuring that repeated values still receive distinct placeholders.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function sqlTag(strings, ...values) {
  let text = '';
  const valuesArray = [];
  let placeholderIndex = 1;

  strings.forEach((str, i) => {
    text += str;
    if (i < values.length) {
      const val = values[i];
      if (val && typeof val === 'object' && val.text && val.values) {
        // Inlined sqlTag result
        const offset = placeholderIndex - 1;
        const inlinedText = val.text.replace(/\$(\d+)/g, (_, num) => `\$${Number(num) + offset}`);
        text += inlinedText;
        valuesArray.push(...val.values);
        placeholderIndex += val.values.length;
      } else if (Array.isArray(val)) {
        // Array becomes parenthesised list of placeholders
        const placeholders = val.map((_, idx) => `\$${placeholderIndex + idx}`).join(', ');
        text += `(${placeholders})`;
        valuesArray.push(...val);
        placeholderIndex += val.length;
      } else {
        // Simple value
        text += `\$${placeholderIndex}`;
        valuesArray.push(val);
        placeholderIndex++;
      }
    }
  });

  return { text, values: valuesArray };
}

module.exports = sqlTag;
```

