I will implement `zipLongest` as a generator function that maintains an array of iterators for each input iterable. For each iteration, it will attempt to pull the next value from each iterator, using the `fill` value when an iterator is exhausted. The generator will only advance each iterator once per iteration, ensuring laziness, and will stop when all iterators are exhausted. Edge cases include handling zero iterables (yielding nothing) and ensuring that once an iterator is exhausted, it is never accessed again.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function* zipLongest(fill, ...iterables) {
  if (iterables.length === 0) return;
  const iterators = iterables.map(it => it[Symbol.iterator]());
  while (true) {
    const row = [];
    let allDone = true;
    for (const it of iterators) {
      const { value, done } = it.next();
      if (done) {
        row.push(fill);
      } else {
        row.push(value);
        allDone = false;
      }
    }
    if (allDone) break;
    yield row;
  }
}

module.exports = zipLongest;
```

