I will use TypeScript’s conditional types and mapped types to enforce that every required property of `Config` must be set exactly once before `.build()` can be called, while optional properties may be omitted. The `Builder` will track which keys have been set via a `Set<keyof Config>` and will narrow the return type of `.set()` using a helper type that removes the key from the “remaining required” set. To make `.build()` callable only after all required keys are set, I’ll use a generic `BuildState` that is `true` only when the set of remaining required keys is empty; the `.build()` method will be defined only in that state, causing a compile‑time error if called earlier. Setting an unknown key or a value of the wrong type will be caught by the type system because the `.set()` method’s parameter types are derived from `Config` and the key must be a valid key of `Config`.

